\chapter{Records and colon calls}
A record maps string keys to values. Literal keys may be identifiers or
quoted strings, and duplicate keys in one literal are a syntax error.
Member access uses a colon; string indexing supports dynamically chosen keys.

\begin{mullcode}
val point <- {x: 3, y: 4, "display-name": "origin offset"};
G:Assert(point:x == 3);
G:Assert(point["display-name"] == "origin offset");
val operations <- {
    square: fn(x){ return x*x; },
    label: "math"
};
G:Assert(operations:square(5) == 25);
G:Assert(G:Len(operations) == 2);
\end{mullcode}

\section{No hidden receiver}
\api{object:member(args)} first selects the member and then calls the resulting
value. It does not inject \api{object} as an extra argument. A method that
needs private state captures it in a closure or native callable. This keeps
record-based APIs identical in C++ and D and makes an extracted callable
behave the same as an immediate colon call.

Records are immutable structural values. Unknown members are errors rather
than implicit nil values. Equality compares keys and values recursively;
display formatting orders keys lexicographically for deterministic output.

An object literal at the beginning of a statement is ambiguous with a block.
Use it as a declaration initializer, return expression, or parenthesized
expression. Package entry points normally return a record containing their
public API, which is why the standard libraries are used through colon calls.
